The knowledge-vault subsystem produces bundles in the Open Knowledge Format, an
open specification published by Google Cloud. OKF defines the portable format:
a directory of Markdown files with YAML frontmatter, `type` as the only required
field, the reserved filenames `index.md` and `log.md`, and cross-links that form
a graph.

OKF does NOT define, and is not the source of, the verification layer this
harness adds on top of it: the `verified_against` commit stamp, the manifest
used as an ID registry and reverse index, the link allowlist, the fixed ordered
body sections, the concept size budget, or a validator that rejects. Those are
this project's work, MIT-licensed, and must not be attributed to Google or to
the OKF specification.

OKF's conformance rules are deliberately permissive and bind consumers, which is
why this project's producer-side strictness is complementary to the spec rather
than a deviation from it. A bundle produced under these stricter rules remains a
conformant OKF bundle.

